function ProfileForm({ profileId }: { profileId: string }) {
  const { data: profile } = useProfileQuery(profileId); // refetches on focus and reconnect
  const [draft, setDraft] = useState<Profile | undefined>(profile);

  useEffect(() => {
    if (profile) setDraft(profile); // ❌ reseeds the whole draft on every background refetch
  }, [profile]);

  if (!draft) return <Skeleton />;

  return (
    <form onSubmit={() => saveProfile(profileId, draft)}>
      <input
        value={draft.displayName}
        onChange={(e) => setDraft({ ...draft, displayName: e.target.value })}
      />
      <button>Save</button>
    </form>
  );
}

scenarios.modelAnswer:
Mechanism. `useProfileQuery` returns a new `Profile` object for every response, so `profile` has a new identity after any background refetch — window focus, reconnect, a `staleTime` expiry, a poll. The effect is keyed on `profile`, so it runs on each of those responses and `setDraft(profile)` replaces the entire draft, including the field being edited. That is why the symptom looks random: it follows network and focus timing, not user actions. Two defects amplify it. The draft is seeded twice — once by `useState(profile)`, which is already `undefined` while the query is in flight, and again by the effect — so there is no single seed moment to reason about. And there is no dirty tracking at all: `saveProfile(profileId, draft)` closes over the same reseeded `draft`, so an edit that arrives between the refetch and the click is dropped silently. Nothing here is a React bug; data-derived state is being recomputed on every data change.

Policy. Separate the baseline from the draft and pick the seed moment explicitly: seed when the record identity arrives — remount the form with `key={profileId}` so another record starts clean, or reset from a handler when the id changes — never from an effect keyed on the data. Track dirty per field, not per form: a form-level `isDirty` cannot answer 'may this field adopt the new value?'. On a refetch, merge server values into untouched fields only. For a field that is dirty and also changed on the server, do not choose silently: mark it 'changed on the server' and let the user keep theirs, take the server value, or reload and reapply. Version the command — send the `updatedAt`/ETag the draft was based on and map a 409 onto that conflict path — instead of last-write-wins. On success the authoritative response becomes the new baseline: reset from it, clear dirty, invalidate the profile query.

A minimal merge, once the baseline lives next to the draft:

```ts
function reconcile(
  draft: Profile,
  baseline: Profile,
  server: Profile,
  dirty: Set<keyof Profile>,
): (keyof Profile)[] {
  const conflicts: (keyof Profile)[] = [];
  for (const key of Object.keys(server) as (keyof Profile)[]) {
    if (!dirty.has(key)) {
      draft[key] = server[key]; // untouched here: adopt the remote value
    } else if (server[key] !== baseline[key]) {
      conflicts.push(key); // edited here and changed remotely
    }
  }
  return conflicts; // surface these, then set baseline = server
}
```

Two boundaries are worth saying out loud. A different record is an identity change, not a refresh: field-by-field merging is only correct for the same id, which is why the `key` remount is the cheap form of that rule. And if the product genuinely wants drafts to survive navigation, that is a persistent-draft feature with its own owner (a dedicated slice or the URL) — not a licence to reseed from query data on every render.

